R["«EXEC»"] = """*(Simulated image embeddings with a hidden true visual identity, built on the documented design of Myntra's ShopLook: detect garments, embed each with a triplet network, retrieve by cosine. Myntra's images, labels and A/B control are not public, so no company number is reproduced. Written after the analysis.)*

- **The question:** can we show visually similar catalogue items for every garment in a model image, which embedding should do it, and what is it worth?
- **A trained embedding wins by a wide margin.** The triplet-trained projection has **NDCG@5 of 0.563** (95% CI 0.556 to 0.570) against 0.351 for the frozen backbone with cosine, 0.348 with Euclidean and 0.134 for in-type best-sellers. Strict P@3 is **45.3%** against 22.3% for frozen cosine. All gaps are significant on the same garments except against the colour histogram on "at least one relevant in the top 5" (McNemar Holm p = 0.33), though its NDCG@5 (0.384) is clearly lower.
- **Whitening alone hurts.** PCA-whitening the frozen embedding drops NDCG@5 to 0.269, below the plain frozen embedding. Unsupervised clean-up does not remove nuisance; supervised pairs do.
- **The detector decides how much of the outfit is covered.** Active learning lifts garments with a relevant recommendation in every group (all Holm p < 0.0001): skirts **51.0% to 68.5%**, bags 67.0% to 75.3%, topwear 80.9% to 86.1%. Outfits where every garment gets a relevant item rise from **49.2% to 59.6%**. Badly cut crops hurt: 90.3% relevant versus 99.0% for clean crops.
- **Strict labels understate relevance.** For the trained embedding strict P@3 is 45.3% but lenient relevance is 79.9%, a gap of 35 points (CI 34 to 35). Our simulated expert review judged **77.4%** relevant (CI 73.5% to 80.8%); it does **not** reproduce the documented 477/500 (95.4%), because our relevance rule is our own.
- **Offline and online disagree on the middle.** Spearman between NDCG@5 and twin click-through is only **+0.09**: the colour histogram ranks second offline but near the bottom online, while best-sellers beat the frozen embeddings on clicks (5.6% vs 4.3%). The trained embedding is best on both (CTR 6.3%, add-to-cart 1.43%).
- **A/B sizing:** a 25% click lift needs about 1,700 users per arm, but a **4% add-to-cart lift needs about 4.7 lakh users per arm** under our twin rates. The documented +4% would be hard to confirm.
- **Money (assumed traffic and margin):** about **₹98 crore a year** over best-sellers in the base case (best ₹141 crore, worst ₹27 crore), before an assumed ₹3 crore running cost. Treat it as an order of magnitude: every rupee input is ours."""
R["«O5»"] = """90 embeddings failed (NaN or all zero) and are excluded from the gallery. The duplicate check (cosine above 0.99) flagged 59 near-duplicate listings, all genuine, and missed 1 of 60. A 400-item label audit found **1.0% wrong article types** (CI 0.4% to 2.5%; we planted about 2%, so the audit sample is a little lucky), and 103 items have no colour. The domain shift is visible: the same item's model photo has mean cosine **0.59** to its catalogue photo and its street photo only **0.48**, and norms grow from 11.8 (catalogue) to 13.8 (model) to 17.7 (street). That gap is why a frozen embedding is a weak retriever and why street-to-shop training pairs matter. The detector misses 1,035 garments (14.4%) even after active learning. The gallery has 5,911 items."""
R["«O61»"] = """the detector covers topwear, bottomwear and footwear well but bags and especially skirts poorly, and active learning narrows the gap most for skirts (**54% to 72%** of garments detected in our simulation). The three image sources have clearly different embedding norms, so catalogue, model and street photos are **not interchangeable** for a frozen model. Types separate in the first two principal components, but those two explain only 34.0% of variance, so most of the structure that matters inside a type (colour, pattern, style) is in other directions. Black, blue and white are 58% of items, so colour-based shortcuts will find many plausible matches."""
R["«O71»"] = """the **triplet-trained embedding is best on every visual metric**: NDCG@5 0.563 (CI 0.556 to 0.570), strict P@3 45.3%, lenient P@5 78.3% and exact-item recall@5 **86.7%**, against 25.2% for the frozen cosine embedding. Euclidean (0.348) and cosine (0.351) are equal for practical purposes. **PCA-whitening hurts** (0.269): it equalises variance across directions that are mostly nuisance. The **colour histogram is a surprisingly strong handcrafted baseline** on lenient relevance (P@5 71.4% against 59.5% for frozen cosine) because our lenient rule rewards colour agreement; but its exact-item recall is only 5.8%. In-type best-sellers are almost never strictly relevant (P@3 3.7%) yet lenient P@5 is still 32.7%, simply because the lenient rule is easy to meet inside a type. Training converged: mean triplet loss fell from 0.011 to 0.002 and the share of active triplets from 6% to 1%."""
R["«O72»"] = """the trained embedding beats frozen Euclidean, frozen cosine, PCA-whitened and best-sellers on the same garments: ΔNDCG@5 = +0.216, +0.212, +0.295 and +0.430 with outfit-cluster CIs that exclude zero by a wide margin, and McNemar Holm p < 0.0001 for each. Against the **colour histogram** the NDCG@5 difference is +0.179 (CI 0.172 to 0.187), but the hit-at-least-once McNemar test is **not significant** (Holm p = 0.33; 126 vs 143 discordant garments): the histogram almost always finds some lenient-relevant item, it just ranks them worse. Intra-outfit correlation is only 0.016 (design effect 1.02), so clustering barely matters here; the bootstrap is a safeguard rather than a big correction. Per-garment NDCG@5 has skewness -0.27, so it is close to symmetric, but it is bounded and discrete, so non-parametric tests were still the safer choice. Wilcoxon p-values are very small but are not cluster-adjusted."""
R["«O73»"] = """active learning raises end-to-end success in all five groups and the gains are paired and significant (Holm p < 0.0001 each; almost every change is a gain, only 1 garment lost, in topwear). The gain is largest where the detector was weakest: **skirts +17.5 points (51.0% to 68.5%)**, bags +8.3, topwear +5.2, bottomwear +3.2, footwear +1.6. Bags and skirts remain the weakest groups. At outfit level the share of images where **every** garment gets a relevant item rises from 49.2% to 59.6%, while at least one garment is covered in 98.0% to 98.9%, so the product is not dead when a detection fails but the full look is. Crop quality matters: relevance is 99.0% for clean crops and 90.3% for badly cut crops (chi-square p = 5e-63). Differences across article types are statistically detectable (p = 0.01) but small (Cramér's V = 0.059), so the main driver is detection and cropping, not which type it is."""
R["«O74»"] = """strict label match understates what a shopper would accept. For the trained embedding, lenient relevance at K = 3 is 79.9% versus strict 45.3%, a **gap of 34.6 points** (CI 33.8 to 35.4); for frozen cosine the gap is 39.0 points (CI 38.2 to 39.7). Both curves fall slowly with K. This reproduces the mechanism behind the paper's low P@3 (28.4) and high expert acceptance, in direction only. Our simulated expert review gave **387 of 500 (77.4%, CI 73.5% to 80.8%)**, with annotator kappa 0.70, against the documented 477/500 (95.4%, CI 93.2% to 96.9%). The documented figure is **higher than ours and outside our CI**; we do not claim to match it, because our lenient rule and the 5% annotator error are assumptions. H3 is rejected: label metrics understate perceived relevance. Use both a strict and a human-judged metric."""
R["«O75»"] = """exact-item recall is the starkest metric. The trained embedding finds the garment's own catalogue item at rank 1 in **69.7%** of cases and in the top 5 in 86.7%, against 8.9% and 25.2% for frozen cosine and 0.2% and 0.6% for best-sellers. On exposure, the trained embedding is healthy: it reaches 92.6% of the gallery, exposure Gini 0.41, and 26.1% of recommendations go to the top 10% of items, with the lowest skewness (1.03). Frozen Euclidean is more hub-prone (Gini 0.49, skewness 2.13). Best-sellers cover under 1% of the gallery (Gini 0.99). So the trained embedding does not buy accuracy by recommending a few hub items. Whether it over-recommends near-duplicates of the worn item (a diversity question) is not measured here."""
R["«O76»"] = """in the twin, the trained embedding is best on both metrics: carousel click-through **6.28%** (CI 6.23% to 6.33%) and add-to-cart rate **1.43%**, versus 5.58% and 0.77% for best-sellers, 4.32% and 0.87% for frozen cosine. That is **+12.5% CTR and +85% add-to-cart over best-sellers**, and about +45% CTR and +64% add-to-cart over frozen cosine. The ordering of the middle methods is not the same as offline: Spearman between offline NDCG@5 and twin CTR is **+0.09 (p = 0.87)**, +0.37 for add-to-cart. The colour histogram looks good offline (NDCG@5 0.384, second) but is nearly worst online (2.1%), and frozen embeddings lose to best-sellers on clicks (popularity pulls clicks in our twin) while winning on add-to-cart. H4 is rejected: offline rank alone is a poor guide except for picking the overall winner. The documented +25% click and +4% add-to-cart have no known control, so our twin lifts neither confirm nor refute them; the twin's click model is assumed."""
R["«O77»"] = """a **25% relative click lift is easy to detect** (about 4,700 carousels, 1,735 users per arm). A 10% click lift needs 27,800 carousels (about 10,200 users). Add-to-cart is the hard metric because it is rare: +10% needs about 77,000 users per arm and the documented **+4% needs about 4.7 lakh users per arm** (12.8 lakh carousels). With 5 lakh carousels per arm the smallest detectable lift is 2.4% for clicks and 6.8% for add-to-cart. So a short test can show the click effect but not a 4% cart effect. H5: the 25% click lift is testable with feasible traffic, the 4% add-to-cart lift only with a long test or a pooled design. These rely on twin control rates (5.6% and 0.77%) that are assumed."""
R["«O78»"] = """brute-force search time grows almost linearly with gallery size (log-log slope 0.85 for 24 dims, 0.84 for 64 dims; R² about 0.96), reaching about 15 to 17 ms per query at 2 lakh items on this machine. That is above the documented 5 ms retrieval, which used a serving stack the paper does not describe. The 24-dimension and 64-dimension spaces cost about the same here, so the compact embedding mainly helps memory. With catalogues of crores of items and per-type indexes, approximate nearest-neighbour search is needed; our timing does not show its recall trade-off."""
